## Behavioral Evidence (when this iteration changes a primary user-mutation flow)

If this iteration adds or modifies a primary user journey — anywhere a user takes an action and expects an observable on-disk or backend effect (create, start, save, publish, delete, attach, approve, cancel, etc.) — capture one driven execution per affected journey into:

    /state/feat-x/run-1/phase-1/iter-2/behaviors/

Use whatever the repo already has: Playwright trace plus screenshot, an AppleScript-driven session log, a headless harness invocation, an HTTP-driven smoke transcript, a Wails event dump, a CLI subprocess transcript with the resulting filesystem diff. The artifact's job is to prove the journey ran end-to-end on the binary the user will actually run, not just that a unit test of an internal helper passed.

Name files after the journey ("create-feature.json", "publish.log", "attach.trace") not the iteration — the iteration directory already carries that context. Include enough context in each artifact to verify the journey completed: the input the test drove, the on-disk or backend state observed afterward, and (if applicable) any error or terminal status. A capture that only shows the binary launched is not behavioral evidence; the user-task must complete.

If this iteration touches no user-mutation surface, the directory's absence is expected.

## Visual Evidence (when UI code changes this iteration)

If this iteration adds or modifies user-facing UI, capture the rendered state of each affected screen into:

    /state/feat-x/run-1/phase-1/iter-2/screenshots/

See skills/frontend-design/SKILL.md (loaded as a mandatory read for this feature) for the full methodology: which harness to use, naming, storage, why it matters. The reviewer on the next hop reads any files it finds in that directory and cannot approve UI changes without them.

If this iteration touches no UI code, the directory's absence is expected.

